# Playwright conventions

## 1. Locators

Use `getByRole`, then `getByLabel` or `getByPlaceholder`, then `getByText`, then `getByTestId` with a comment on why a higher option failed; when several elements match, narrow with `.filter({ hasText })`.

✅ Good — `pages/ProgramsPage.ts`, `pages/LoginPage.ts`:

```typescript
this.newProgramButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: '+ New Program' });
this.email = page.getByLabel('Email');
this.signInHeading = page.getByRole('paragraph').filter({
  hasText: 'Sign in to your account',
});
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
page.locator('button.mantine-Button-root').first();
page.locator('//button[contains(text(), "New Program")]');
```

Why: CSS, XPath, and `.first()` bind the test to markup order, so a restyle or a second matching control clicks the wrong element.

No `getByTestId` call exists in this repo. Use it only as the last resort, with a comment naming which role, label, and text failed.

## 2. Waiting

Wait with web-first `expect(locator).toBeVisible()`, `.toBeEnabled()`, or `.toHaveText()`; a known network boundary may use `waitForResponse` for that method and path.

✅ Good — `tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts`, `pages/ProgramsPage.ts`:

```typescript
await expect(programsPage.heading).toBeVisible({ timeout: 30_000 });

const programsLoaded = this.page.waitForResponse(
  (response) =>
    response.url().includes('/api/programs') &&
    response.request().method() === 'GET' &&
    response.ok(),
);
await this.page.goto('/programs');
await programsLoaded;
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
await page.waitForTimeout(3000);
await page.waitForLoadState('networkidle');
```

Why: A fixed sleep and `networkidle` pass or fail with machine speed, and they never name the control or response the test is actually waiting for.

## 3. Page Objects

One class per page or distinct component under `pages/`; locators are `readonly` fields, methods such as `goto`, `clickX`, `fillY`, and `submit` perform actions only, every `expect` stays in the spec, and a page that contains a component holds that object (`new XxxPage(page)`).

✅ Good — `pages/ProgramsPage.ts`, `pages/NewProgramModal.ts`:

```typescript
this.newProgramModal = new NewProgramModal(page);

async openNewProgramForm(): Promise<void> {
  await this.newProgramButton.click();
}
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
async openNewProgramForm(): Promise<void> {
  await this.page.locator('#new-program').click();
  await expect(this.page.getByRole('dialog')).toBeVisible();
}
```

Why: An assertion or a raw selector inside the page object hides a failed open behind a helper and duplicates the same locator across specs.

`page.route` stays in the spec. Page objects do not stub the network or install the clock.

## 4. Auth

Stored session is the default: the `setup` project runs `tests/auth.setup.ts` once and writes cookies and localStorage to `playwright/.auth/user.json`; login-page tests start empty; API login (POST credentials, then inject the token) is only for bulk setup, one-off scripts, or a stale storage state.

✅ Good — `tests/auth.setup.ts`, `tests/ds4-delete-program.spec.ts`:

```typescript
await page.context().storageState({ path: authStorageStatePath });

const context = await browser.newContext({
  storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] },
});
```

Authenticated projects keep `storageState: 'playwright/.auth/user.json'` and `dependencies: ['setup']`. A whole file that must start logged out can use `test.use({ storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] } })`.

❌ Bad:

```typescript
test.beforeEach(async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(process.env.DIDAXIS_EMAIL!);
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign In' }).click();
});
```

Why: A UI login before every feature test turns a slow Sign In into a product failure and multiplies session flake across the suite.

## 5. Test data

Tests that create a program import `test` from `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts`, call `trackProgram(uuid)` as soon as the id is known, and name records with `uniqueName`; the fixture deletes with `DELETE /api/programs/<uuid>` using `process.env.DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN`.

✅ Good — `tests/helpers/programs.ts`:

```typescript
export function uniqueName(base: string): string {
  return `${base} ${Date.now()}`;
}

const createResponse = programsPage.page.waitForResponse(isProgramCreateResponse);
await programsPage.createProgram(name, description);
await trackCreateFromResponse(await createResponse, trackProgram);
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
test.afterAll(async () => {
  await fetch('https://test.didaxis.studio/api/programs/11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111', {
    method: 'DELETE',
    headers: { Authorization: 'Bearer eyJhbGciOi...' },
  });
});
```

Why: An `afterAll` with a hardcoded token or a foreign id either leaves rows on the shared tenant or deletes a program the test did not create.

Do not `trackProgram` an id from a `page.route` fulfill. A mocked create did not persist a row. Never commit `.env`.

## 6. API testing

Assert the real Programs API with Playwright's `request` fixture, `baseURL` from `DIDAXIS_URL`, and `Authorization: Bearer ${process.env.DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN}`; check the status and the body shape.

No feature spec does this yet. `tests/purge-programs.manual.spec.ts` opens `request.newContext` only to delete rows by hand. Advise this for an API check:

✅ Good:

```typescript
test('GET /api/programs returns a program array', async ({ request }) => {
  const token = process.env.DIDAXIS_API_TOKEN;
  const response = await request.get('/api/programs', {
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${token}` },
  });
  expect(response.status()).toBe(200);
  expect(Array.isArray(await response.json())).toBe(true);
});
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
const response = await request.get('/api/programs');
expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy();
```

Why: A call without the bearer token, checked only with `ok()`, treats 401 and 500 as the same boolean and never proves the list payload.

Leave this call unrouted. Mocking the endpoint under test belongs in section 7 and voids the check.

## 7. Network mocking

Fulfill `/api/programs` with `page.route` in the spec, matched by method and path, and drive every click and navigation through `ProgramsPage`, `NewProgramModal`, and `EditProgramModal`; never mock the endpoint that test is verifying, and assert only copy read from the live page.

No spec calls `page.route` yet. Advise this for an empty list:

✅ Good:

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/programs', async (route) => {
  if (route.request().method() !== 'GET') {
    await route.continue();
    return;
  }
  await route.fulfill({
    status: 200,
    contentType: 'application/json',
    body: '[]',
  });
});
const programsPage = new ProgramsPage(page);
await programsPage.goto();
await expect(programsPage.heading).toBeVisible();
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
await page.route('**/api/**', (route) => route.fulfill({ status: 503, body: 'nope' }));
await expect(page.getByText('Something went wrong')).toBeVisible();
```

Why: A blanket fulfill stubs the create or delete the test meant to hit, and an invented sentence passes while the app shows different copy or nothing.

One case per test. Fulfill only the response whose UI handling is under test: `GET /api/programs` for the list, `POST /api/programs` for create, and the update request the edit form sends for save. Do not fulfill `DELETE /api/programs/<uuid>`. Before writing an assertion string, open the real programs page and read the error and empty-state sentence. If the app renders nothing, say so in the test.

Fulfill these, each in its own test:

- Save `503` — the UI error state, using copy read from the app.
- List `200` with `[]` — the empty-state message, using copy read from the app.
- List `200` with a malformed body (not a program array) — the app does not crash; assert visible copy only when the app shows it.
- List or save `401`, `403`, `404`, `400`, `409`, `500`, `501`, `502`, `504`, `204` with an empty body, and `300` — the copy read from the app, or an explicit note that the app renders nothing. For `204`, `300`, and malformed `200`, also assert the page stays up.

## 8. Accessibility

Cover the programs page in `tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts` with one tag per test: an axe scan for `wcag2a` and `wcag2aa` asserted with `expect(results.violations).toEqual([])`, plus a keyboard path axe cannot do (tab to the primary control, `toBeFocused()`, Enter, dialog open), through existing page objects only.

✅ Good — keyboard path in `tests/programs.a11y.spec.ts`; assertion shape required for the scan:

```typescript
await programsPage.tabToNewProgramButton();
await expect(programsPage.newProgramButton).toBeFocused();
await page.keyboard.press('Enter');
await expect(modal.dialog).toBeVisible();

const results = await new AxeBuilder({ page })
  .withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa'])
  .analyze();
await expect(results.violations).toEqual([]);
```

❌ Bad — `tests/helpers/a11y.ts` returns without failing when `CI` is set:

```typescript
if (process.env.CI) {
  testInfo.annotations.push({ type: 'axe-violations', description: summary });
  return;
}
new AxeBuilder({ page }).withTags(['wcag2a', 'wcag2aa']).disableRules(['color-contrast']);
```

Why: A CI early return or `.disableRules()` keeps the job green while the page still fails WCAG.

Scope with `.include()` / `.exclude()` only if a third-party widget is noisy, and comment why. If the scan finds real violations, report them and stop.

## 9. Assertions

Assertions are web-first and live in the spec: `expect(locator).toBeVisible()`, `.toBeEnabled()`, `.toBeDisabled()`, `.toHaveText()`, or `.toBeFocused()`; assert the label the action produces, and do not change what an assertion checks to cure a flake.

✅ Good — `tests/ds1-create-program.spec.ts`:

```typescript
await expect(modal.dialog).toBeVisible();
await expect(modal.createButton).toBeDisabled();
await expect(programsPage.programEditButton(programName)).toBeVisible();
```

❌ Bad:

```typescript
expect(await programsPage.heading.isVisible()).toBe(true);
expect(programsPage.programEditButton(programName)).toBeTruthy();
```

Why: A one-shot `isVisible()` or a truthy locator reads the DOM once before the list paints, so the same spec fails or passes with no retry.

## 10. Anti-flake

Keep the runner contract, freeze any relative timestamp in the spec before navigation, and fix a flake by changing locators and waits only.

`playwright.config.ts` already sets `fullyParallel: true`, `retries: process.env.CI ? 2 : 0`, and `trace: 'on-first-retry'`. Do not raise `retries` above 2. Do not set `workers`, including `workers: 1`. `use.baseURL` stays `process.env.DIDAXIS_URL`. `use.locale` and `use.timezoneId` are not named in this repo; ask the user before writing either value.

No spec installs a frozen clock yet. Advise this when the assertion is a relative label such as `5 minutes ago`:

✅ Good:

```typescript
await page.clock.install({ time: new Date('2026-11-01T15:00:00.000Z') });
await programsPage.goto();
await expect(programsPage.programRow(programName)).toContainText('5 minutes ago');
```

The call stays in the spec, not in a page object. Assert the label that instant produces.

❌ Bad:

```typescript
retries: 5,
workers: 1,
await expect(page.getByText(/minutes ago/)).toBeVisible();
```

Why: Extra retries and a single worker hide a race, and a relative label follows the wall clock so the same spec fails after the timestamp rolls.

## Do / Don't

| Do | Don't |
| --- | --- |
| `getByRole`, then label, placeholder, text, then `getByTestId` with a why comment | CSS, XPath, `.first()`, or brittle text |
| `.filter({ hasText })` when several elements match | Clicking the first match in DOM order |
| `expect(locator).toBeVisible()` / `.toBeEnabled()` / `.toHaveText()` | `waitForTimeout`, `networkidle`, or `expect(await locator.isVisible()).toBe(true)` |
| Actions in `pages/`; `expect` in the spec | Inline locators in a spec, or `expect` inside a page object |
| `storageState` from `auth.setup.ts` for feature tests | UI login in every `beforeEach` |
| `test.use({ storageState: { cookies: [], origins: [] } })` for login-page tests | Reusing the saved session to test Sign In |
| `trackProgram(uuid)` from `fixtures/cleanup.fixture.ts` and `uniqueName` | `afterAll` cleanup, a hardcoded token, or deleting a row the test did not create |
| `request.get` / `request.post` with status and body asserts for API checks | `expect(response.ok()).toBeTruthy()` with no bearer token |
| `page.route` matched by method and path, copy read from the app | Mocking the endpoint the test verifies, fulfilling `DELETE`, or inventing error text |
| `expect(results.violations).toEqual([])` and a keyboard path | `.disableRules()` or a CI path that swallows violations |
| `page.clock.install({ time })` before navigation for relative timestamps | Asserting `minutes ago` against the wall clock |
| `retries` of `process.env.CI ? 2 : 0`, no `workers` key | `retries` above 2 or `workers: 1` |
